   "B": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the agriculture and markets law, in relation to the humane destruction or disposition of certain animals; and repealing certain provisions of such law relating thereto",
    "basePrintNo": "A999",
    "basePrintNoStr": "A999-2009",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Rpld §374 subs 2-a, 2-b, 2-c, 2-d & 2-e, amd §§374, 332 & 118, Ag & Mkts L",
    "lawSection": "Agriculture and Markets Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:A999B\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the agriculture and markets law, in\nrelation to the humane destruction or disposition of certain animals;\nand repealing certain provisions of such law relating thereto\n\nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL: To ensure that when it is necessary to\neuthanize an animal, it is done in the most humane manner possible\n\nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS: Section one repeals subdivisions 2-a,\n2-b, 2c, 2-d and 2-e of section 374 of the agriculture and markets law.\n\nSection two amends subdivisions 1 and 2 of section 374 of the agricul-\nture and markets law, as added by chapter 545 of the laws of 1971, by\nreplacing \"the American Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Animals\nor any society duly incorporated for that purpose\" with \"a duly incorpo-\nrated society for the prevention of cruelty to animals, or any dog\ncontrol officer\" as those who may lawfully cause to be humanely\ndestroyed certain specified animals if, upon examination, a licensed\nveterinarian certifies in writing that an animal is so maimed, diseased,\ndisabled or infirmed so as to be unfit for any useful purpose and humane\neuthanasia is warranted; or two reputable citizens who are called by an\nagent or officer of a duly incorporated society for the prevention of\ncruelty to animals or a police officer make the same finding. Addi-\ntionally, an animal may be lawfully and humanely destroyed if such\nagent, officer or police officer obtains written consent from such\nanimal's owner. Subdivision two provides that in the absence of such\nfindings or certificate, a duly incorporated society for the prevention\nof cruelty to animals, or any pound maintained by or under contract or\nagreement with any county, city, town or village may make available for\nadoption, or humanely destroy, an animal after five days, unless the\nanimal is redeemed by its owner.\n\nSection three renumbers subdivisions 3, 4, and 5 of section 374 of the\nagriculture and markets law 6, 7, and 8, respectively. New subdivisions\n3, 4, and 5 are added. Paragraph a of new subdivision 3 provides that,\nunless in cases of emergency where euthanasia of an animal by gunshot is\npermitted pursuant to subdivision 4 of this section, euthanasia of an\nanimal shall be accomplished solely by means of injection of sodium\npentobarbital or sodium pentobarbital solution administered by a certi-\nfied euthanasia technician, a licensed veterinarian or a licensed veter-\ninary technician. Whenever euthanasia is to be performed by an intracar-\ndiac injection of sodium pentobarbital or sodium pentobarbital solution,\nit must be performed only on an animal that is heavily sedated, anesthe-\ntized, or comatose. Only a licensed veterinarian may perform euthanasia\nby intracardiac injection of sodium pentobarbital or sodium pentobarbi-\ntal solution on an animal that is not heavily sedated, anesthetized, or\ncomatose and only when such veterinarian determines that such euthanasia\nis the most humane option available. When euthanasia is performed by\nintracardiac injection of sodium pentobarbital or sodium pentobarbital\nsolution on an animal that is not heavily sedated, anesthetized, or\ncomatose, the veterinarian must document the administration of such\n\ninjection and the reason for it in writing. Such documentation shall be\nretained for at least three years. Under no circumstances shall intra-\ncardiac injection be performed on animals that are not heavily sedated,\nanesthetized or comatose when an animal is under the care of any duly\nincorporated society for the prevention of cruelty to animals or pound.\n\nParagraph b of subdivision 3 provides that no animal shall be left unat-\ntended between the time the euthanasia procedure begins and death is\nconfirmed. The body of a euthanized animal shall not be disposed of in\nany manner until death is confirmed by specified persons. A violation of\nparagraph b shall be punishable by a civil penalty of not more than five\nhundred dollars. The state department of health shall promulgate regu-\nlations necessary for implementation of the provisions of this subdivi-\nsion including those governing the training and certification of certi-\nfied euthanasia technicians. Paragraph a of new subdivision 4 provides\nthat any other method of euthanasia is prohibited except euthanasia by\ngunshot is permissible as an emergency measure when an animal is posing\nan imminent threat of serious physical injury to a person or to another\nanimal and where the use of a humane method of euthanasia that is\nprescribed above is rendered impossible, or in cases where a severely\ninjured animal is suffering and cannot otherwise be aided. Paragraph b\nof new subdivision 4 provides that any chamber used to induce hypoxia by\nmeans of a lethal gas shall be dismantled, rendered inoperable and\nbeyond repair, and removed from the premises within ninety days of the\neffective date of this legislation. Violation of paragraph b of new\nsubdivision 4 shall be punishable by a civil penalty of not more than\nfive hundred dollars. New subdivision 5 provides that no dog or cat\nshall be released from the custody or control of any pound, shelter or\nother places listed in this subdivision, unless for the purpose of\nadoption or redemption by its owner, and a violation of such subdivision\nor subdivision two, three or four of section 374 shall constitute a\nmisdemeanor punishable by imprisonment for not more than one year, or by\na fine of not more than one thousand dollars, or both.\n\nSection four amends paragraphs b and e of subdivision 8 of section 374\nof the agriculture and markets law, as added by chapter 569 of the laws\nof 1995, by renumbering subdivisions two-a, two-b, two-c and two-d\nsubdivisions two, three, four and five respectively.\n\nSection five amends section 332 of the agriculture and markets law, as\namended by chapter 674 of the laws of 1980 by providing that when an\nanimal is euthanized, it shall be in accordance with the provisions of\nsection three hundred seventy-four of this chapter.\n\nSection six amends subdivisions seven and seven-a of section 118 of the\nagriculture and markets law by providing that subdivisions two-a, two-b,\ntwo-c, two-d and two-e shall no longer be applicable because this legis-\nlation repeals such subdivisions.\n\nSection seven provides the effective date.\n\nJUSTIFICATION: This legislation simply updates the outmoded New York\nState law governing euthanasia of animals to ensure that, when it is\nnecessary, euthanasia is performed in the most humane manner possible.\nTo this end, the bill requires that, except in clearly outlined and\nlimited circumstances, euthanasia shall be performed by injection of\nsodium pentobarbital or sodium pentobarbital solution by a licensed\nveterinarian, euthanasia technician or veterinary technician on an\nanimal that is heavily sedated, anesthetized or comatose.\n\nImportantly, this legislation clearly provides that when euthanasia by\nintracardiac injection of sodium pentobarbital or sodium pentobarbital\nsolution is to be performed on an animal that is not heavily sedated,\nanesthetized, or comatose, it is to be performed only by a licensed\nveterinarian, and he or she must document the event and the reason(s)\nfor it.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY: A.5399-C 2008, passed assembly. A.5399-A,\n2008, passed assembly. A,5399-A 2007, passed assembly. A.12055, 2006\nreferred to agriculture committee.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATION: None to the state.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE: This act shall take effect on the three hundred sixty-\nfifth day after it shall become a law.\n\n",
